// Mission 01 — Getting Eyes on Your Perimeter: KQL Queries
// All queries executed against Log Analytics workspace: log-sentinel-lab
// Data sources: Heartbeat (agent connectivity), Event (unstructured), SecurityEvent (structured)
// Machine: WKS-L57.hamilton.corp
// Date: October 7, 2026

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 1: Verify Agent Connectivity (Heartbeat Check)
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Confirm that the Azure Monitor Agent is communicating with Log Analytics.
// This is the first diagnostic query for any "no data" problem.
// Result: Shows machine is sending heartbeats, agent is functioning.

Heartbeat
| take 10

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 2: Machine Health Status
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Check when the machine was last seen by Log Analytics.
// Indicates whether the machine is currently online/sending data.
// Result on 10/7/2026: WKS-L57 last seen at 11:54 AM (current at query time).

Heartbeat
| summarize LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated) by Computer

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 3: Data Freshness / Ingestion Latency
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Verify that data is arriving in near-real-time (not delayed).
// Calculates delay between event occurrence and Log Analytics ingestion.
// Result: Data is current, minimal ingestion delay.

Heartbeat
| project TimeGenerated, ingestion_time(), Delay = ingestion_time() - TimeGenerated
| take 20

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 4: Event Type Distribution
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Understand what types of events are being collected.
// Breaks down events by severity level: Critical, Error, Warning.
// Useful for baseline understanding of system health.

Event
| summarize count() by EventLevelName

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 5: Security Logon Events (Unstructured - Event Table)
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Query logon events from the unstructured Event table.
// Event ID 4624 = Successful account logon.
// Note: Data is embedded in RenderedDescription field (requires string parsing).
//
// This query demonstrates data collection from dcr-windowsevents
// (Chain A: generic Windows Event Log collection).
// Result: Logon events returned, but data requires KQL string parsing for analysis.

Event
| where EventLog == "Security" and EventID == 4624
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, RenderedDescription
| order by TimeGenerated desc

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 6: Security Logon Events (Structured - SecurityEvent Table)
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Query logon events from the structured SecurityEvent table.
// Event ID 4624 = Successful account logon.
// Data arrives pre-parsed with dedicated columns: Account, LogonType, IpAddress, etc.
//
// This query demonstrates data collection from dcr-securityevents
// (Chain B: Sentinel-optimized Security event collection).
// Result: Logon events with structured data, ready for Sentinel detection rules.
// Status at time of execution (13:48): Created, awaiting 15-30 min ingestion time.

SecurityEvent
| where EventID == 4624
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, Account, LogonType, IpAddress
| order by TimeGenerated desc

---

// ============================================================================
// QUERY 7: Failed Logon Attempts (Detection Use Case)
// ============================================================================
// Purpose: Detect failed logon attempts (Event ID 4625).
// Useful for identifying brute-force or unauthorized access attempts.
// Data comes from SecurityEvent table (structured).

SecurityEvent
| where EventID == 4625
| project TimeGenerated, Computer, Account, FailureReason, IpAddress
| order by TimeGenerated desc

---

// ============================================================================
// NOTES ON DATA COLLECTION CHAINS
// ============================================================================
//
// Chain A (dcr-windowsevents → Event table):
// - Collects: Application, System, Security (before change), Windows Event Logs
// - Data format: Unstructured text in RenderedDescription field
// - Use case: General IT operations monitoring
// - Status: Reduced to Application + System only (Security moved to Chain B)
//
// Chain B (dcr-securityevents → SecurityEvent table):
// - Collects: Security logs only (via "Windows Security Events via AMA")
// - Data format: Pre-structured columns (Account, LogonType, IpAddress, etc.)
// - Use case: Security operations, Sentinel detection rules
// - Status: Active, awaiting data ingestion at time of documentation
//
// Rationale for consolidation:
// Both chains were initially collecting Security logs = double cost.
// Consolidated to Chain B (SecurityEvent) for SOC-optimized data structure.
// Chain A now handles Application/System only.

---

